# 4. AUTHENTICATION AND ACCESS CONTROL

Real authentication is mandatory.

Do not build a demo login.

Do not provide a hardcoded username and password.

Do not allow unauthenticated users to enter protected pages.

Do not simulate successful authentication in the frontend.

## 4.1 Authentication

Inspect the capabilities of the current development environment and implement a real, supported authentication system.

Prefer an established authentication provider that integrates securely with the available stack.

If Firebase is supported, Firebase Authentication is an acceptable option. Otherwise, use an equivalent supported provider.

Implement the supported features necessary for the application:
- Account creation.
- Sign-in.
- Sign-out.
- Session persistence.
- Protected routes.
- Appropriate authentication error messages.
- Password recovery where supported.
- Secure account and session handling.

Never store plaintext passwords in application data.

Never put private service-account credentials, privileged API keys, or server secrets in client-side code.

If provider setup requires external configuration, identify the exact configuration required. Do not pretend the integration is complete until it actually works.

## 4.2 Organization access

ORIGIN must support organization-scoped workspaces.

A user must only access organizations to which they have been explicitly granted membership.

An organization must not be accessible merely because a user knows its URL or organization ID.

Implement server-side authorization for protected data and actions.

Frontend route guards improve the user experience but are not a security boundary.

Every protected backend operation must independently verify the authenticated identity, organization membership, and required permissions.

