# 14. TECHNICAL ARCHITECTURE

Before implementation, inspect the actual capabilities and structure of the current Google AI Studio project.

Choose a stack that can genuinely run in the available environment.

Keep the application modular and maintainable.

The architecture must separate:

1. User interface.
2. Authentication and session management.
3. Authorization.
4. Data access and persistence.
5. CSV ingestion and validation.
6. Inventory calculations.
7. Recommendation generation.
8. Scenario simulation.
9. Reporting and export.
10. Audit and observability.

Use a supported frontend framework and a real backend or server-side execution layer for protected operations.

Python with pandas is a reasonable option for data processing when the environment supports it. A TypeScript or JavaScript backend is also acceptable if it can implement the same calculations correctly.

Do not force an unsupported technology into the project merely because it appears in this specification.

Do not put privileged operations exclusively in the browser.

Do not place service credentials in frontend environment variables that are exposed to users.

Keep calculation functions independently testable.

Use explicit types and validation schemas where supported.

Separate data models, business logic, persistence, and UI components.

Avoid giant components that contain the entire application.

Avoid duplicated calculation formulas across pages.

All pages must use the same authoritative calculation logic.

Provide clear error handling and structured logs that do not expose sensitive information.

